  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Discovery — Cloud Service / Account Discovery", "technique": "T1651", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 08:02:11 CEST, Wazuh's AWS CloudTrail module logged an sts:GetCallerIdentity API call attributed to IAM principal 'iam-user/svc-deploy' with sourceIPAddress=185.220.101.33 (a Tor exit node, ASN 205100) and userAgent 'aws-cli/2.13.x Python/3.11 Linux'. svc-deploy is a CI/CD service account whose access keys are expected to be used ONLY from the build pipeline egress IP — never interactively from aws-cli, and never from a Tor exit. GetCallerIdentity is the classic 'whoami' an attacker runs first after loading stolen keys to confirm which principal the keys map to. Wazuh fired rule 92959 ('AWS CloudTrail: API call from unexpected source IP for service account').", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'amazon' and 'aws' and rule.description matched 'CloudTrail' / 'GetCallerIdentity' with the call attributed to svc-deploy from a non-baseline external sourceIPAddress. The univariate signature: a CI/CD service-account key used interactively (aws-cli userAgent) from a brand-new geo within the same minute as the rest of the chain.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery — Cloud Infrastructure Discovery", "technique": "T1651", "what_happened": "At 08:06:30 CEST (~4 minutes after the whoami), the same principal/source issued ssm:DescribeInstanceInformation to enumerate the SSM-managed EC2 fleet in region eu-west-1. This lists every instance the keys can reach via Systems Manager — the attacker is mapping the blast radius before executing. The call appears in CloudTrail whether it succeeds (returns the fleet) or fails with AccessDenied. Wazuh fired rule 92961 ('AWS CloudTrail: SSM fleet enumeration from unexpected principal').", "chain_reason": "rule.description matched 'SSM' / 'DescribeInstanceInformation' with rule.groups containing 'aws' and the call attributed to iam-user/svc-deploy from sourceIPAddress 185.220.101.33. The GetCallerIdentity → DescribeInstanceInformation sequence within minutes is the canonical cloud lateral-recon pattern that precedes command execution.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:06:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution — Cloud Administration Command", "technique": "T1651", "what_happened": "At 08:10:55 CEST, the same principal/source issued ssm:SendCommand with documentName 'AWS-RunShellScript' targeting 'tag:Env=prod' — i.e. it attempted to run a shell script ('id', 'uname -a', 'cat /etc/passwd | head -5') across EVERY production EC2 instance carrying that tag. This is high blast-radius remote command execution through the legitimate SSM control plane — no malware, no inbound network, just abuse of the cloud admin interface. CloudTrail records the SendCommand event with the document name, target tag and parameters regardless of whether the principal had ssm:SendCommand permission. Wazuh fired rule 92960 ('AWS CloudTrail: SSM RunCommand SendCommand from unexpected principal — AWS-RunShellScript to prod fleet').", "chain_reason": "rule.id 92960 fired with rule.description matching 'SSM RunCommand' / 'SendCommand' / 'AWS-RunShellScript', rule.groups containing 'amazon' and 'aws', rule.mitre.id T1651, attributed to iam-user/svc-deploy from sourceIPAddress 185.220.101.33. The GetCallerIdentity → DescribeInstanceInformation → SendCommand sequence from a single Tor source within ~9 minutes is the textbook stolen-key cloud-administration-command kill chain.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T08:10:55+0200"}
